# 1. Definition

### Interview-ready definition

> **Object-Oriented Programming is a programming paradigm that models software as interacting objects that encapsulate state and behavior, using abstraction, encapsulation, inheritance, and polymorphism to achieve modularity, reuse, extensibility, and maintainability.**

But for an **advanced interview**, don't stop at the four pillars.

The real OOP stack is:

```text
                 OOP
                  │
       ┌──────────┼──────────┐
       ↓          ↓          ↓
 Encapsulation Abstraction Polymorphism
       │          │          │
       └──────────┼──────────┘
                  ↓
          Object Relationships
                  │
       ┌──────────┼──────────┐
       ↓          ↓          ↓
 Inheritance Composition Association
                  ↓
              SOLID
                  ↓
        Design Patterns
                  ↓
       Production Architecture
```

---

# 2. Why Does OOP Exist?

Suppose you build an e-commerce application.

Without proper abstraction:

```text
OrderService
    │
    ├── knows UPI implementation
    ├── knows Card implementation
    ├── knows database details
    ├── knows email implementation
    └── knows SMS implementation
```

This creates **tight coupling**.

Changing one implementation can affect many components.

Good OOP attempts to create:

```text
Business Logic
      │
      ↓
Abstraction / Interface
      │
 ┌────┼─────┐
 ↓    ↓     ↓
UPI  Card  Wallet
```

The business logic depends on the **contract**, not the concrete implementation.

This connects OOP directly to:

> **Loose coupling + Dependency Inversion + Dependency Injection**

