# 9. ⭐ Abstract Class vs Interface

| Feature              | Abstract Class           | Interface                   |
| -------------------- | ------------------------ | --------------------------- |
| Constructor          | Yes                      | No                          |
| Instance variables   | Yes                      | No ordinary instance fields |
| Abstract methods     | Yes                      | Yes                         |
| Concrete methods     | Yes                      | Yes                         |
| Multiple inheritance | No                       | Multiple interfaces         |
| `static` methods     | Yes                      | Yes                         |
| `default` methods    | No special requirement   | Yes                         |
| Private methods      | Yes                      | Yes, modern Java            |
| Main purpose         | Shared abstraction/state | Contract/capability         |

### Design rule

Use an **abstract class** when subclasses share:

* state
* implementation
* common lifecycle

Use an **interface** when you primarily need:

* a contract
* interchangeable implementations
* multiple capabilities

